     "videoId": "cWFjOK9sfBw",
     "channel": "YourGermanTeacher (Luzi)",
     "start": 0,
     "end": 571,
     "note": "Chapters: 1:54 when to use the Perfekt · 2:52 when to use *haben* · 3:57 how to form the past participle · 4:26 regular verbs · 8:17 verbs in *-ieren*.\n\nThis video has no usable subtitles — follow the slides on screen. The examples in this lesson are standard German for the same rules."
    },
    {
     "type": "note",
     "title": "The Perfekt: how Germans talk about the past",
     "md": "In **spoken** German the Perfekt is *the* past tense: {de:Was hast du gestern gemacht?} = What **did** you do yesterday?\n\n**haben** (conjugated, position 2) + **past participle** (at the end):\n\n{de:Ich **habe** Deutsch **gelernt**.} · {de:Gestern **habe** ich Deutsch **gelernt**.}\n\nMost verbs use **haben** — especially verbs with an Akkusativ object. (A few use *sein* → lesson 35.)"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "title": "Regular verbs: ge + stem + t",
     "skipFirst": true,
     "cols": [
      "verb",
      "past participle",
      "example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "machen",
       "ge|mach|t",
       "Ich habe … gemacht."
      ],
      [
       "lernen",
       "ge|lern|t",
       "Ich habe Deutsch gelernt."
      ],
      [
       "kaufen",
       "ge|kauf|t",
       "Ich habe Brot gekauft."
      ],
      [
       "spielen",
       "ge|spiel|t",
       "Wir haben gespielt."
      ],
      [
       "arbeiten",
       "ge|arbeit|et",
       "Er hat gearbeitet."
      ],
      [
       "telefonieren",
       "telefonier|t",
       "Ich habe telefoniert."
      ],
      [
       "studieren",
       "studier|t",
       "Was hast du studiert?"
      ]
     ],
     "note": "Stem in *-t / -d* → **-et**. Verbs in **-ieren** get **no ge-**."
